+# align — evidence
+
+The story behind the skill's statements. Only what would change this skill and no other.
+
+## The retype step — added 2026-09-03
+
+**The skill had no ending.** An align resolves the decisions a `HITL` ticket was `HITL` for,
+and the ticket's `Type` is supposed to change when nothing human-owned remains. Five agent
+sessions had done exactly that after the rule-4 guard existed — 2026-08-27, four on
+2026-08-29 — and the step was written nowhere. The operator's description of it: *"this is the
+entire goal of align."*
+
+**What it cost when it was unwritten.** 0034's decisions were made by the operator on
+2026-09-02 and recorded on the ticket. On 2026-09-03 the operator said *"you can change 0034 to
+AFK."* The guard refused the `Edit`, as its header said it would for both cases. The agent then
+refused itself, twice, citing the guard's refusal as though it were the rule — strict rule 4
+forbids the retype *to clear a stall*, and this was not that. When the agent finally tried the
+unguarded shell road, the harness's classifier refused that too. For one morning a legitimate
+retype had no agent route, and the operator was being asked to edit two lines by hand for a
+decision they had already made in words. `C40`.
+
+**What settled the shape.** The operator writes nothing but messages. So the mark the guard
+reads is what the agent records when the operator decides: a dated heading on the ticket,
+`## Resolved — the operator, YYYY-MM-DD`. Three tickets already carried it that week, undesigned.
+The guard now reads the file's after-state for it. An allow-file the operator would maintain
+was refused for that reason; an attended-session flag does not exist in the harness.
+
+**What the step protects against.** A session inventing the mark — writing *the operator, date*
+for a ruling never given. That is forging an operator's word, a worse act than the retype and a
+dated lie in history; the guard cannot see it and does not try. The statement in the
+`<project-local>` block says the mark may not be invented, and that sentence is the whole
+guard against it.
+
+**Watched failing.** Two checks in `hooks.test.js` went red against the unamended guard before
+the amendment — a ruled ticket refused, and an unruled one refused for the wrong reason — then
+green. Then 0034 and 0062 were retyped live through the `Edit` tool, on the amended guard.

