+---
+name: maintain
+description: >-
+  Maintain a mechanism whose rules or records have gone stale, or build up one that is
+  missing a part the shape requires. Use when a wake reports a mechanism due, when a
+  triage pass picks a due row or an unmarked mechanism, and whenever what governs a
+  mechanism has moved since it was last checked.
+---
+
+# Maintain
+
+**A maintenance repairs.** A pass that ends by naming what someone should do has not
+maintained anything, and the mark it moves is false.
+
+What a mechanism is and the shape one must reach: `/mechanism`. A maintenance **is** an amend —
+that skill holds the mechanics, this one holds the order.
+
+## The pipeline `[bone]`
+
+`/mechanism` → the instance's doc and rules → its records. Each governs the next.
+
+**Survey — what is already known, before looking for anything new**
+
+| # | step | how |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **gather the mechanism's open issues** | tickets by their `Mechanism:` field **and** from its register row's `tickets` cell — the cell is transcribed by hand and has missed maintenance tickets written the day before; failures, concerns and misses by the same field, or by grep where the backfill has not reached |
+| 2 | **find its current discrepancies** | the repair table below |
+
+**Plan — only where there is work**
+
+| # | step | how |
+|---|---|---|
+| 3 | **mint a maintenance ticket** through `/ticket`, carrying the plan | skip entirely when steps 1–2 found nothing; move the marks and stop |
+| 4 | **fold in the pre-existing tickets you are resolving** | `close-ticket.js <slug> --fold-into=<maintenance>`, and the maintenance ticket must name each. Fully absorbed leaves; partly absorbed stays open with the moved criteria struck through |
+
+**Repair — in order, and never stop at the step you were sent for**
+
+| # | step | when |
+|---|---|---|
+| 5 | **build up** — supply every required part that is missing | any part absent, whatever the trigger |
+| 6 | **amend** the doc and rules to `/mechanism`'s current shape | `/mechanism` moved since the `rules` mark |
+| 7 | **`/denoise`** the doc | 5 or 6 wrote anything |
+| 8 | **amend** the records to that doc and those rules | the instance's surfaces moved since the `output` mark, or 6 or 7 changed them |
+| 9 | **`/denoise`** the records | 8 wrote anything |
+
+**Close**
+
+| # | step | how |
+|---|---|---|
+| 10 | **move each mark reached**, then hand the ticket on | `Grading: awaiting — built by session NNNN`, which `grading-due.js` announces to a later session |
+
+Skip a step with nothing to do; never reorder. **Marks: `rules` after 7, `output` after 9** — a
+`/mechanism`-triggered maintenance runs the whole repair phase and moves both.
+
+**Survey precedes discrepancy on purpose.** Going looking before reading what is registered is
+how a session re-derives a diagnosis the tree already wrote down.
+
+## Doing it
+
+1. **Read the dueness from `maintenance-due.js`**, never infer it. `Rules edited out-of-band`
+   is the rules level.
+2. **Derive what changed at each step** — step 6 reads `/mechanism` against the doc, step 8
+   reads that doc and its rules against the records.
+3. **Repair as you go.**
+4. **Move each mark you reached**:
+   `node .agents/scripts/maintenance-mark.js <slug> <rules|output>`.
+
+## Repair, by what you find
+
+- **A stale claim** — correct it. Where the stale copy restated a fact homed elsewhere,
+  **delete rather than update**.
+- **A missing `maintain.js`** — write the record's contract, asserted. **Format never content,
+  live rows only**; archived history exempt.
+- **A missing `extract.js`** — one line exporting the deriver.
+- **A missing doc, moments table or register row** — `/mechanism`'s *Incept* step 7.
+- **A rule with no home** — into `<slug>.rules.md`, then install.
+- **Prose said twice** — `/denoise`, whose rules stay its own.
+
+## Records
+
+**Mainline, by script.** A record with a checkable contract is maintained by its
+`<slug>.maintain.js`. Author-distance does not apply to a format check.
+
+**Where no maintainer exists, writing it is the maintenance** — not reading the rows.
+
+**A trace log is never read *whole*.** A bounded slice, stating the bound, for the two questions
+a process cannot answer: writing that maintainer, and judging a row it reported. **200 rows**,
+and say which 200 — head, tail, or a filter and its predicate. The number is a working bound
+delegated by the operator (2026-09-03) rather than one they set: it is two screens, enough to
+see a format and its exceptions, and small against the 149,711 rows `hooks.log` held that day.
+
+**Where a record cannot be scripted**, the reading needs a party that did not write it. Say so.
+
+## What defers a repair
+
+**A decision only a person may take, and nothing else.** The deferral names the decision, never
+the ticket: meta-rule 6 governs who may *grade* ticketed work, never who may write it.
+
+<project-local>
+Dueness is announced by `.claude/hooks/maintenance-due.js` and is a `/triage` input, ranked by
+cost like any backlog row. **The never-maintained count is pickable too** — a mechanism with no
+mark produces no dueness line, so picking one from that count is the only road to its first
+mark that is not an amend.
+
+The register is `mechanisms/mechanism-shape/mechanism-shape.register.md`; the marks are
+`memory/maintenance-marks.tsv`, written only by `maintenance-mark.js`. N — the churn threshold
+— is the operator's, recorded in `mechanisms/maintenance/maintenance.doc.md`.
+
+**A write that reports success and changes nothing is a failure**, named at the turn boundary
+and registered before it is retried (`/failure`).
+</project-local>
+
+→ [EVIDENCE.md](./EVIDENCE.md)

