+# mechanism — evidence
+
+Why the statements in `SKILL.md` are there. Kept beside the skill so the instruction stays
+an instruction.
+
+Written 2026-08-27, session 0022, in an `/align` with the operator. **Nothing here has been
+measured.** Every statement is either a rule already carried elsewhere and moved to a moment
+that fires, or a shape the operator named from specimens. The one claim genuinely new to this
+file — that a build skill would have prevented them — is untested and is stated as such at
+the bottom.
+
+Entries carry ids `[E1]`…, allocated in order and never reused, so `SKILL.md` can cite a
+number rather than restate an argument — the convention this file's own skill states.
+
+## The hole was named by two tier-1 descriptions before anything filled it [E1]
+
+Not an invention. Two skill descriptions, forced into every session's context, each hand off
+to *building a mechanism* and neither owns it:
+
+- `/experiment` — *putting a mechanism on trial **rather than simply building it***
+- `/failure` — *decides whether it **earns a mechanism***
+
+So the corpus has said, at its most expensive tier, twice a session, for days, that there is
+an act called building a mechanism. Nothing was that act. `/triage` completed the shape by
+routing to one: *the skill that owns it — `/ticket` to mint, `/implement` to build.*
+
+## Why `/implement` was not it, and is untouched [E2]
+
+`/implement` is unported reference-corpus software delivery: *alchemical cyber/steampunk*
+naming, `/tdd` (which does not exist here), typechecking, `/sync-arch` review. It mentions no
+meta-rule, no layer, no seam, no strike, no grading.
+
+**And it carries a rule this repo killed.** `memory/skills.md` records *"Do not commit unless
+got explicit instruction"* as withdrawn 2026-08-24, noting it was *stated three times across
+`commit` and `implement`*. `/commit` was fixed. `/implement` still ends *"Do not commit your
+work until requested."* A statement in the graveyard, removed from one of its two homes and
+alive in the other for three days — which is the same defect this skill's *derive the reach*
+rule exists to prevent, sitting inside the file that should have carried the rule.
+
+**Kept anyway, at the operator's direction**, along with `/sync-arch`: both become useful
+against a real codebase, and this corpus is not one. Repairing them into corpus skills would
+have destroyed two working artifacts to avoid writing one file.
+
+**The description is why it never fired.** *Implement a piece of work based on a spec or set
+of tickets* reads as software delivery and matched nothing this session did while shipping a
+code fix, a check, three tickets and two skill changes. The operator's diagnosis, and it
+relocates the defect from the body to the one line that is paid by every session whether or
+not the skill is used.
+
+## Where each statement came from [E3]
+
+| statement | origin |
+|---|---|
+| the reliance test — *anything something else relies on as a source of instructions or behaviour* | the operator's wording, 2026-08-27. It replaced *will a later session be bound by this*, which only saw sessions and missed that programs rely too — a hook on `settings.json`, a check on a directory layout |
+| name the seam and both ends **before** writing either | meta-rule 2, moved from diagnosis to prevention. The rule already existed and fires today only after the fact, through `readers-named.js` and post-hoc analysis |
+| expand the shape before acting | [0028](../../../docs/tickets/0028-reasoning-failure-class.md), the operator's naming 2026-08-26, nine specimens |
+| watch it fail, both polarities | meta-rule 3 |
+| name what will grade it, and it is not you | meta-rule 6 |
+| name what retires it | `/skill-up`'s *a mechanism that only adds is incomplete*, and the cap-is-not-a-removal clause, which is that skill's and is referenced rather than restated |
+| derive the reach, never remember it | session 0021's reading, plus two further instances the following day |
+
+**Nothing here is new law.** Every line is a rule this corpus already holds, moved to the
+moment it would fire. That is the claim to check: if the rules were already right and only
+their delivery was wrong, this skill should change outcomes without changing any rule.
+
+## Why one skill with two faces rather than two skills [E4]
+
+The operator's, 2026-08-27. Incepting and amending share every governing statement — a seam,
+an expansion, a layer, a watched failure, a grader — and differ only in whether the thing
+already exists. Two skills would have carried one list twice, which is the split home
+meta-rule 7 forbids, in the file arguing against split homes.
+
+## Why the amend face does not collide with triage's sweep [E5]
+
+Both read standing mechanisms, so the boundary had to be argued rather than assumed.
+`/triage`'s inward sweep asks **is this worth keeping** — could it be generalised,
+simplified, dropped — rotates regions, proposes, and never cuts, because it is deciding what
+earns the next unit of work. This skill asks **is this sound and still covered**, and repairs
+what it finds. Same file, different question, and only the second one is triggered by a
+change.
+
+The gap that proves the second is not covered: 2026-08-27, the metrics layer entered
+`layers.js` and every surface restating it went stale within hours — `project-map.js`
+silently dropped a whole layer, `core/README.md` said four layers and listed five while the
+classifier held six. No sweep would have found that, because nothing there was *unneeded*.
+
+## The allowlist, and the guard that did not catch me stating it — 2026-08-27 [E6]
+
+**The rule is the operator's and it is an allowlist:** instructions stay, compact
+representative examples stay, everything else goes to the sidecar. It replaced *remove the
+story*, which is the same rule stated as a denylist — and a denylist must enumerate what to
+exclude, so it is always short. That reasoning is here rather than in the body for two
+reasons: it is the story of how the rule was worded, and the fact that the guard implements a
+denylist is a property of `story-prose.js`, which says so itself. A skill restating it would
+be a second home for a fact about a mechanism it does not own.
+
+**The body carried that argument for one commit**, inside the very clause defining the rule.
+The operator caught it. Two things follow, and the second is the useful one:
+
+- `skill-scope.js` was run against the offending sentence afterwards and stayed **silent**.
+  No pattern matches *that is the same rule stated as a denylist*.
+- So the false-negative class documented in `story-prose.js` that morning was demonstrated
+  the same day, on this file, by its own author — and the only thing that caught it was a
+  person reading. **That is the measured shape of the guard's coverage**, and it is why the
+  incept face says a clean run means nothing was caught rather than that the artifact obeys.
+
+Trimmed in the same pass, by reading the body against the allowlist rather than the patterns:
+an argument that records would get *ceremony and catch nothing*, and a restatement of
+meta-rule 2's coincidence in place of a reference to it.
+
+## Why strength stays inline and provenance does not [E7]
+
+The body said *provenance stays*, and the operator asked why it is not a reference like
+everything else — then sharpened it: **the strength of a rule is useful to the agent
+instructed by it; the provenance is not.** That is right, and the word had been carrying
+three things:
+
+- **strength** — thin, or settled. Decides whether you follow or push back. Inline.
+- **authority** — who set it, who may change it. Decides whether you may amend it at all.
+  Inline, and in this corpus it is often the same token as the attribution.
+- **provenance** — which comment, which incident, which thread. Says where to go and check,
+  and following the pointer *is* the checking. Referenced.
+
+Meta-rule 8 says *cite it and say it is thin, where the rule is used*, which bundles the
+first two with the third. It also already half-concedes the split: *Strong: the evidence is
+retained but the rule need not carry it.* `metrics/README.md` requirement 9 draws the line
+the same way — *Origin* is a field naming a ticket or post with its author, not a paragraph.
+
+**Scoped here, proposed there.** This skill states the split for what it builds; the
+amendment to meta-rule 8 is marked `[meta?]` in `docs/rules-evidence.md` and is the
+operator's through `/align`, per meta-rule 9. Nothing here adopts it.
+
+**Untested, and the untested part is specific:** that a pointer gets followed as reliably as
+an inline citation gets read. Nobody has measured either.
+
+## Why an id and not a heading link — 2026-08-27 [E8]
+
+The operator's, while the strength/provenance split was being written: **the sidecar gives
+each entry an id and the rule references the number.** Adopted as an ordinary rule of this
+skill on three arguments, one of which is the only one that matters:
+
+1. **It makes the pointer checkable.** *See the sidecar* cannot be verified; `[E7]` can. That
+   is the whole difference between a convention and a mechanism, and this repo's own record
+   is that a convention without a reader is not delivered.
+2. **It gives [0040](../../../docs/tickets/0040-regrade-horizon-table.md) per-entry
+   granularity.** Its outcome is *a statement whose evidence has gone stale is flagged*,
+   measured as *days since the evidence last moved* — computable per file today, per
+   statement with ids.
+3. **It survives retitling**, which a heading link does not.
+
+**Two design calls, both to avoid a worse failure than the one being fixed.** The check runs
+one direction only: a cited id must resolve, an uncited entry is not a defect — requiring
+citation would make a graveyard entry illegal. And ids are never reused, because a recycled
+id resolves *silently to the wrong entry*, which is worse than dangling. Nothing can enforce
+the second; it is stated where a sidecar is edited.
+
+**Its first run failed on this file's own skill**, which cites `[E1]` and `[E2]` as worked
+examples of the form — character-identical to citations. `story-prose.js` had already solved
+that shape for fenced and inline code, so it was borrowed rather than re-solved.
+
+**The retrofit is not done and the number is the honest part:** 14 sidecars, 5 entries with
+an id, **58 without**. Recorded rather than fixed, per this skill's own rule that a new
+mechanism is run over what already exists and the backlog is either cleared or counted. The
+pass belongs with [0041](../../../docs/tickets/0041-instruction-not-story.md)'s piece 3.
+
+## What is untested, and what would refute it [E9]
+
+- **That a build skill prevents the failures it is written from.** Every specimen it cites
+  was diagnosed after the fact by a person or a hook, and none was prevented by anything.
+  This file's whole thesis is delivery rather than content, and delivery is exactly what has
+  not been observed.
+- **Refuted if:** the skill is invoked and the next one-ended seam ships anyway, or the
+  sessions that ship mechanisms do not invoke it — which is `/implement`'s failure returning
+  under a better description, and the more likely of the two.
+- **Confirmed if:** a session names a consuming end it would not otherwise have written, or
+  an amend pass finds a stale reliance nobody reported.
+- **The trigger is the fragile part, not the body.** It has one instance of evidence — the
+  operator's diagnosis of why `/implement` never fired — and a description is judged by what
+  it matches, which no one here has measured for any skill.
+
+## The blacklist beside the positive test [E10]
+
+*What counts* carried **a record of what happened is not a mechanism** from this skill's
+first draft, and `CLAUDE.md` meta-rule 10 inherited it on adoption, 2026-08-27.
+
+**It was redundant where right and wrong where it bit.** The positive test — *does something
+else rely on this as a source of instructions or behaviour* — already excludes a one-off
+record, which is consulted by nothing. But `memory/failures.md` **is** a record of what
+happened, `failures-open.js` is documented as its mechanical reader, and `mechanisms/mechanism-shape/mechanism-shape.register.md`
+lists it as M06's **sidecar** — the third shape a mechanism owes. Read literally, the
+blacklist excluded a file the register was simultaneously listing as part of a mechanism.
+
+**The distinction it was reaching for is writing versus register.** Writing an entry belongs
+to the skill owning that entry; the register defining how entries are written and read is a
