+---
+name: mechanism
+description: >-
+  Incept or amend a mechanism — anything something else relies on as a source of
+  instructions or behaviour: a hook, a check, a script, a skill, a rule, a layer
+  contract. Use before building one, when a shape has moved so what relied on it may
+  now be stale, or to review existing mechanisms. Not for records of what happened.
+---
+
+# Mechanism
+
+**Incept** builds something others will rely on. **Amend** returns to one already standing —
+its shape moved, or you want to know whether it still holds — and re-checks everything
+relying on it.
+
+Not how to write code. The conventions that outlive whoever ran it. Why any of it:
+[EVIDENCE.md](./EVIDENCE.md).
+
+## What counts
+
+**Does something else rely on this as a source of instructions or behaviour?** Reliance, not
+code. A rule nobody is routed to fails the test. A directory layout three programs read
+passes it.
+
+**A system, never a file** — one spans a hook, a skill, a library, a register, a wake line.
+
+**Records sit inside mechanisms**: a register is the third shape and its readers are parts.
+Writing an *entry* is not mechanism work — that belongs to the skill owning the entry `[E10]`.
+
+## The three shapes `[bone]`
+
+Meta-rule 10 routes here and states none of this; **this is its only home.**
+
+| shape | holds | read when | story |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **doc** | what it **is** and what it is **for**; how it works, install/uninstall, **its moments**, **every rule it injects and where**, and **it names the instruction files** | installing, removing, amending, debugging — never in ordinary work | **no** |
+| **instruction file** | the rules that get injected. Current state only. **Usually a skill, and there may be several** | in the work it governs | **no** |
+| **sidecars** | history and data | when the trace is the question | yes |
+
+**Only the sidecar takes story.** A doc excludes why the shape is this, what it cost, what was
+refuted, what it used to be, and when each changed — **one sidecar for all of it** *(the
+operator, 2026-08-30)*, `<slug>.evidence.md`. Decisions a person made stay in the owning
+ticket's `## Resolutions`.
+
+**Doc and instruction file are never the same file** (the operator, 2026-08-27) — checked by
+`mechanisms-shape.js`. Whether they may share a **sidecar** is open and the operator's.
+
+### Injection and retraction `[bone]`
+
+**A mechanism does not always get its own instruction file. It injects rules into skills that
+already exist** *(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. M08 and M12 have named two instruction surfaces
+each since before this was written down — the practice preceded the rule.
+
+- **The rules live in `mechanisms/<slug>/<slug>.rules.md`, one demarcated section per rule,
+  each naming its own target** *(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. That file is their only home.
+  A copy in a target is installed, never authored.
+- **The injector is generic**: `.agents/scripts/inject-rules.js <slug>`. One script for every
+  mechanism — per-mechanism installers holding rule text is the shape this replaced, and it
+  put the rules in two places while this page asserted the wrong one.
+- **Injection is mechanical in both directions or it is not injection.** A rule added by hand
+  is retracted by whoever remembers it, which is nobody.
+- **Retraction removes the clause and never the host.** The skill it was injected into
+  pre-existed the mechanism and outlives it.
+- **Of the two retractions, `<slug>.uninstall.js` defaults to pause and disable is asked for**
+  *(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. A retraction whose bare invocation destroys data is one someone
+  runs once by accident. **The scope is the uninstaller and only the uninstaller** *(the
+  operator, 2026-08-29)* — this sentence stood unscoped here, `inject-rules.js` implemented it
+  as its own default across install and retract alike, and a bare invocation therefore
+  retracted. **`inject-rules.js` has no default mode and names one or refuses.**
+
+**Turning a mechanism off is two different acts, and the difference is the data**
+*(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. Both retract every injected rule and unregister every hook;
+**neither deletes the definition, ever.**
+
+| act | injected rules | hooks | accumulated data | the definition |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **pause** / **stop** | retracted | unregistered | **kept** | stays in `mechanisms/<slug>/` |
+| **disable** | retracted | unregistered | **deleted** | **folded into a separate folder, never deleted** |
+
+**Pause is for a mechanism that will run again** — its series continues where it stopped, and
+a gap in the data is a fact about the pause rather than a hole. **Disable is for one that has
+finished**: nothing will read its rows again, which is the question that decides between them.
+
+**The doc keeps every injected rule while the mechanism is off**, in both acts. That is what
+makes turning it back on mechanical rather than a reconstruction, and it is why the register
+row and the doc survive a disable that removes everything else.
+
+Anything that *truly* leaves the tree is `/denoise`'s and `/triage`'s to decide, never a side
+effect of either act.
+
+**Deleting the data and deleting the definition were one act until this split.** They are not
+the same question: nothing reads a dead mechanism's rows, and the definition is the only thing
+that says what it was and what it decided. Disable answers both correctly and neither did
+before.
+
+**A mechanism may spawn promises** *(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. An obligation with a moment
+after this session belongs in `memory/promises.md` where a wake will find it, carrying the
+mechanism's slug; uninstalling releases them in writing rather than dropping them.
+
+**Tier 1 carries the trigger; tier 2 carries the instruction.** An instruction file: **its
+doc names it**, and **something in tier 1 routes to it**. A doc: **the register routes to
+it** and tier 1 does not, so `readers-named.js`'s INSTRUCTION verdict does not apply.
+
+## Moments `[bone]`
+
+**A doc lists every moment at which a person acts on this mechanism, and what instructs them
+there.** One row per moment. *Does it have an instruction file* is the wrong question — M03
+answered `none` while one of its four moments was instructed and three were not.
+
+A moment with no instruction file carries **which kind of none**, because three are fine and
+one is a gap. **Every row states why, in a clause** — the label alone is a verdict, and a
+verdict nobody can check is what the `doc` column's tick was.
+
+| kind | meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **elsewhere** | instructed, by another mechanism. **Name it.** |
+| **embedded** | an instruction exists, in a hook's output string or a code comment. Covered in practice, wrong home. |
+| **unowned by design** | something surfaces to a person and the correct response is nothing. Say so, or it reads as an oversight forever. |
+| **not yet** | it wants one and nobody wrote it. **The only gap**, and it names a ticket — **never the mechanism's own migration ticket**, which closes when the mechanism reaches the shape and cannot carry a change of behaviour. Checked. |
+
+**`elsewhere` and `embedded` are the two that get missed**, and they pull opposite ways: the
+first looks like a gap and is not, the second looks covered and is not.
+
+**There is no *none needed*.** If nobody acts, it is not a moment and the row does not exist.
+That value was in this list for one commit and every candidate for it turned out to be
+`embedded` — the instruction was in the wake line all along.
+
+## The mechanism directory `[bone]`
+
+**Everything but the working parts lives in one directory** — doc, lifecycle scripts and
+sidecars alike. Working parts stay where the harness needs them: a hook must live in
+`.claude/hooks/`.
+
+```
+mechanisms/<slug>/
+  <slug>.doc.md        what it is, how it works. Names the instruction files
+  <slug>.rules.md      the rules it injects, one section per rule, each naming its target
+  <slug>.install.js    touchpoints that are NOT injected rules. Absent when there are none
+  <slug>.uninstall.js  the same touchpoints, removed. Pause is its default, disable is asked for
+  <slug>.extract.js    the paths a clean copy takes, read by M16 `extract-core.js`. Absent = none
+  <slug>.evidence.md   the story — why this shape, what it cost, what was refuted
+```
+
+**Injected rules need no per-mechanism scripts** — `inject-rules.js` handles them from
+`<slug>.rules.md`. Lifecycle scripts are for the rest: a `settings.json` entry, a directory,
+a registration somewhere. A mechanism whose only touchpoints are rules has neither, and that
+absence is a property rather than a gap.
+
+**Some mechanisms are always on and cannot be installed or uninstalled** *(the operator,
+2026-08-28)*. M00 — the shape itself — is one: nothing can turn off the thing that defines
+what turning off means. Their shape is **complete without lifecycle scripts**, and the
+register says so rather than leaving a reader to wonder which of the two it is.
+
+Slug, never the `M<n>` number — numbers move, slugs do not. **The filename repeats the
+slug**: twenty-one bare `install.js` are indistinguishable in a stack trace, a grep hit or a
+process list.
+
+**Two kinds of sidecar, and they live in different places** *(the operator, 2026-08-27)*:
+
+| sidecar | holds | layer | home |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **about the mechanism** | why this shape, what was refuted | core | `mechanisms/<slug>/<slug>.evidence.md` |
+| **accumulated by it** | this instance's history and data | record | **`memory/`, carrying the slug** |
+
+An accumulating record must not sit in the folder: `mechanisms/` is `core` in `layers.js`, so
+a clean copy would carry this instance's entries. It **carries the slug in its filename**, so
+the tie to its mechanism is visible without opening it.
+
+**A backlog is a ratchet, never a threshold.** Where a mechanism ships with a standing
+backlog, count it and hold the count: silent at or below the mark, loud when it **rises**. A
+mark is **lowered** freely as the backlog clears — that is what progress looks like — and
+**raised only by a stated change of definition, with the cause written beside the number.** A
+re-baseline and the drift the mark exists to catch are otherwise indistinguishable. A hook
+that announces a standing backlog every wake is noise, and noise is what gets routed around.
+
+**A part may be claimed by several mechanisms** — a system shares parts, and asserting
+uniqueness fails the tree on every legitimate sharing. Uniqueness is a real question only for
+`extract.js`, where two mechanisms claiming one path is a genuine conflict. **A file that
+belongs to no mechanism goes in an allowlist with its reason**, never left silent: an
+allowlist is how a gap stays declared instead of invisible.
+
+**A shared file used by a non-removable mechanism belongs to that mechanism, and must not
+appear in any relying mechanism's install, uninstall or extract paths** *(the operator,
+2026-08-28)*. **Several users, exactly one claimant** — and this says which, so the tie is
+settled by a property rather than by whoever migrated first.
+
+The always-on mechanism is the safe owner because it is the one that cannot leave. A reliant
+mechanism naming the path anyway is not a duplicate entry; it is an **uninstall that removes
+something nothing may remove**, and it would report success. Extract fails the other way: both
+copy it, and the agreement between them is a coincidence rather than a design.
+
+**The constraint is on the scripts, not on the doc** *(the operator, 2026-08-28)*. A dependent
+mechanism's doc **should** name the file — the reliance is real and a reader needs it — marked
+as relied on rather than owned, and naming the owner. What may not touch it is
+`install.js`, `uninstall.js` and `extract.js`.
+
+Documenting and acting are different jobs, and only one of them runs. A reader who is told
+*this mechanism needs `backlog-marker.js`, which M06 owns* knows both facts; a script that
+lists it knows only the second and acts on it.
+
+**Only `extract.js` can be checked for this today, and the reason is worth knowing.**
+`mechanisms-shape.js` reads extract claims because an extract script *declares* paths and
+returning them is safe. An installer *performs*; requiring one from a wake hook would run it.
+So the rule binds all three and is enforced on one — which means **install and uninstall must
+expose their touchpoints as data rather than only acting on them**, or this rule stays prose
+for two of the three surfaces it names → [0044.0220](../../../docs/tickets/0044.0220-mechanism-shape-lifecycle.md).
+
+**Every accumulating record states its contraction, or carries a standing ticket for the
+absence** *(the operator, 2026-08-27)*. Where entries go when they are finished with, and what
+removes them — M20's subject. A record that only grows becomes one nobody reads, and then the
+mechanism has an audience of zero while looking healthy. `memory/failures.md` archives to
+`failures-done.md` and is the shape; `memory/archive/hooks.log` is at 62,957 lines with
+nothing that evicts, and is not.
+
+**A doc, or a sidecar in the wrong one of those two homes, is unmigrated — not exempt.**
+`memory/failures.md`, `memory/misses.md` and `memory/archive/hooks.log` are accumulating
+sidecars that do not carry their mechanism's slug; each rename is its own migration ticket's
+work, never a sweep.
+
+**Install and uninstall are the same touchpoints in both directions**, so write the list once
+and let both scripts read it. A mechanism installs and uninstalls whole; there is no partial.
+
+**`extract.js` decides what a clean copy takes, and it is peeled off `layers.js` one
+mechanism at a time.** `extract-core.js` keeps listing every tracked file and keeps refusing
+when a file is claimed by nobody; a mechanism with no `extract.js` yet falls back to
+`layers.js`. What is still in `layers.js` when the last one lands is the measurement of what
+belongs to no mechanism.
+
+**Two mechanisms claiming one path is a conflict and is reported.** `layers.js` resolves that
+case silently by list order — its own header warns that reversing two entries ships the
+operator's aliases into a clean copy.
+
+**One check covers all of them**, table-driven over `mechanisms/mechanism-shape/mechanism-shape.register.md`: every `extract.js`
+returns paths that exist, that no other mechanism claims, and that `layers.js` does not
+contradict. Write it with the *first* migrated mechanism. Sixty-three bespoke checks would be
+a larger corpus than the thing they test.
+
+**Story is kept, not deleted — it moves to `<slug>.evidence.md`.** Provenance is not story
+and stays (meta-rule 8). While a mechanism's instruction file is a skill, that skill's
+`EVIDENCE.md` is doing this job; whether the two are one sidecar is the open sharing question.
+
+**Check the exception first:** a shape whose core substance *is* trace takes no split —
+session record, evidence file, register, ticket.
+
+**A mechanism may be tagged `experimental`** — its implementation is settled and its *worth*
+is not, so it carries a registered ending and a default of abandon. Marked in the register's
+name cell, like `meta-mechanism`. Built and experimental are different axes: `Done` on a
+ticket is consistent with a running experiment on what that ticket built.
+
+**Settled 2026-08-28 by the operator, and it was larger than a tag.** An experiment **is** a
+mechanism wearing it, so `experiments/` is not a layer: each running trial becomes a register
+row, and a trial is the clearest case of a mechanism that injects rules into skills it did not
